			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "e3f8a765-1142-31bd-4b52-a41e52cda133")
	)
	(text_box "WHY THE 74LVC1G80\nThe simplest D flip-flop there is: D, CLK,\none output and the two rails, and nothing\nelse. No preset and no clear, so there is no\nasynchronous input that could reset a\nsampler which must never be reset, and\nnothing to tie off.\nSame 74LVC family and the same SOT-23-5\nfootprint as the squarer and the Schmitt, so\nthe whole board is one package type at\nassembly."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 330.2 167.64 0)
		(size 62.23 31.75)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "e4ffcd86-491c-bd9d-9837-fe319dfd7221")
	)
	(text_box "SCHMITT BUFFER, NOT AN INVERTER.\nHysteresis is wrong for the 10 MHz path - it moves the trigger off the zero\ncrossing and turns amplitude into phase - but it is right here: a pps is a rare,\nfast, possibly noisy edge on a long cable, which is the chatter case hysteresis\nexists for.\nNo self-bias: a Schmitt sets its own thresholds and must not be biased into its\nown linear region.\nTimestamped in the FPGA twice: against the helper counter (100 ns, the same grid\nas the beats) and, from gateware v1.1, against the 100 MHz PS clock (10 ns). The\n10 ns one is what tells two channels' 10 MHz phases apart by whole 100 ns cycles\nafter a power cycle or a dropped edge; the picoseconds come from the beat.\n(Rounds 10-27 said '200.02 MHz to 5 ns'; no such clock was ever built.)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.78 358.14 0)
		(size 121.92 34.29)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "f9acde52-de49-af27-1fe3-fcf825df911d")
	)
	(text_box "THE SAMPLING FLIP-FLOP (FF1) IS DELIBERATELY ALLOWED TO GO METASTABLE - that is\nmeasurement.  Never put a synchronizer in front of it.\nTHE RETIMER (FF2) re-clocks on the next helper edge, so what leaves the board is\nalways a clean transition aligned to a helper edge.  It costs exactly one helper\ncycle of latency and destroys no phase information.\nTHE 74LVC1G80 OUTPUT IS INVERTED, so the two stages restore the sense.  Both\ndatasheets NAME pin 4 'Q' in the pinout drawing and only say otherwise in the\ntext: TI SCES221T calls it 'Inverted output' and its Table 7-1 gives CLK up, D =\nH -> Q = L; Nexperia rev 17 Table 4 agrees.  Pinout confirmed from both: 1 D, 2\nCLK, 3 GND, 4 Q(inv), 5 VCC."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.78 204.47 0)
		(size 121.92 29.21)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "f9b6b7e0-0ca4-afe2-d581-cecba559e3d4")
	)
	(text_box "DC-COUPLED, DELIBERATELY.\nThe pps widths in this inventory span 1 us (FE-5680A) to 100 ms (ZED-F9T,\nLBE-1421), and no single AC-coupling RC covers five decades: a 10n/100k network\npasses the 1 us pulse and droops away to nothing 1% into the 100 ms one.\nThe 1k/10k divider is fitted on EVERY pps channel and suits a 3.3 V and a 5 V\nsource alike: 3.00 V and 4.55 V at the gate, both comfortably above the\nSchmitt's VT+ and below its 5.5 V recommended maximum.  74LVC inputs are 5 V\ntolerant on a 3.3 V rail, so the divider is there to keep the swing sane rather\nthan merely survivable -- and to keep every channel identically populated."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 358.14 0)
		(size 121.92 26.67)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "ff8ca7f5-7ec4-61db-9141-c41ba4d3712d")
	)
